# BearVision 3 open work

- Calibrate BearTag motion/RSSI thresholds from labelled field clips.
- Run end-to-end hardware tests with GoPro preview, capture, BLE and Box.
- Benchmark each selected Windows or Linux Edge computer with GoPro preview, YOLO and BLE active together.
- Build one synchronized scenario bundle from an existing test video, detections and approximately 10 Hz BearTag data.
- Proxy the real GoPro preview through Edge Control and add authentication before remote network exposure.
- Verify model and test-video redistribution rights before a public release.
- Select the BearVision source-code licence before distribution.
- Add opt-in verification for credentialed Box and physical hardware.

## Architecture review follow-up (2026-08-26)

### P0 - Make the camera capture window explicit and verifiable

- [x] Document the agreed capture contract: the preview frame timestamp is the
  authoritative detection time; the requested interval is detection time minus
  configured HindSight through detection time plus post-detection duration.
- [x] Preserve the original GoPro file unchanged as the raw clip. Record both the
  requested interval and the actual interval delivered by the camera so processing
  can trim deterministically and timing differences remain visible.
- [x] Clamp missing pre-roll to the camera's earliest available media after startup.
  Later detections during an active capture must not extend its end time.
- [x] Return the actual captured start/end (or equivalent window metadata) from
  the `Camera` port instead of returning media without timing evidence.
- [x] Make GoPro, simulated GoPro and simulated camera validate their effective
  window, including 0/15/30-second requests and start-of-timeline clamping.
- [x] Add shared camera contract tests that fail on configured/requested window
  mismatches and validate downloaded media duration within an explicit tolerance.
- [x] Update `specs/components/clip-extraction.md`; remove the stale statement
  that version 3.0 always issues zero pre-roll.

### P1 - Use one Edge capture-to-publish pipeline

- [x] Extend the orchestration result/event surface with the processing and trace
  metadata needed by scenario evaluation; do not expose mutable implementation
  fields such as `detector` and `observations` to runners.
- [x] Configure `VideoScenarioRunner` with the same `ClipProcessor` and publishing
  path as hardware execution.
- [x] Remove the runner's second processing, time adjustment, package hashing and
  `queue.publish()` implementation.
- [x] Add parity tests proving hardware-style and recorded-video execution produce
  equivalent manifests, observation windows, ordering and publish behaviour.
- [x] Preserve ADR 0007: `process_server=False` must never run the server worker
  in-process.

### P2 - Reduce Scenario duplication after the pipeline is unified

- [x] Extract shared rider-registry/worker assembly, trace ordering, result
  construction and expectation evaluation from the two scenario runners.
- [x] Keep the synthetic and recorded-video execution loops separate unless the
  resulting shared interface is smaller and clearer than both loops.
- [x] Add composition tests for supported component-source combinations. The
  disk-backed GoPro is already selectable as `simulated_gopro`; do not reimplement it.

### P3 - Prove queue semantics before adding another abstraction

- [x] Add one reusable `JobQueue` contract suite covering READY-last publication,
  idempotency, claim/resume, finish, requeue and normalized snapshots.
- [x] Run it against Box, filesystem and memory queues; align known drift such as
  processed-user validation and normalized in-memory snapshot status.
- [x] Extract pure package serialization and snapshot formatting helpers where the
  contract tests demonstrate identical behaviour.
- [x] Defer a provider-independent durable queue state-machine module until the
  low-level provider seam is demonstrably smaller than the current `JobQueue` port.
